  "id": "DrugBank_DB00366 - MONDO_2258",
  "verbalization": "Doxylamine is implicated in the pathogenesis of pharyngitis through a chain of indications that ultimately lead to the condition. The evidence suggests that Doxylamine is associated with Nasopharyngitis, which in turn is linked to other substances that indicate Pharyngitis. Specifically, Nasopharyngitis is connected to Bromodiphenhydramine, Guaiacol, and Ammonium Chloride, all of which are indicated to cause Pharyngitis. This suggests a potential pathway where Doxylamine may indirectly contribute to pharyngitis through its association with Nasopharyngitis and the subsequent indications of other compounds that also contribute to the condition. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible mechanism by which Doxylamine could be linked to pharyngitis, though the exact nature of this relationship remains to be fully elucidated.",
